    "field": "Hydroclimatology",
    "problem": "Existing global climate studies rarely consider the compounding impacts on water availability by jointly analyzing seasonal and annual variations in both precipitation and evaporation from a non-parametric statistical standpoint.",
    "method": "A non-parametric framework is proposed to analyze seasonal hydroclimatic regimes by classifying global land regions into nine categories based on historical precipitation means and seasonality, which are then used to assess future changes in water availability projected by climate models.",
    "task": "The task is to assess future water availability by analyzing compounding changes in mean and seasonal precipitation and evaporation within nine distinct global hydroclimatic regimes, using CMIP5 climate model projections.",
